    referenceDetails: { article: '4.30', section: '4.30.4.1; 4.30.4.2(a)-(d)', table: '4.30.4.2' },
    quickAnswer: 'The motor branch fuse or breaker protects against short circuits and ground faults and must also carry motor starting current. For an ordinary motor, determine PEC full-load current under §4.30.1.6(a)(1), identify the exact protective-device type and motor type, and multiply by the applicable percentage in Table 4.30.4.2. Do not use overload percentages. Permitted increases for starting are conditional and capped, while manufacturer overload-relay tables or equipment markings can impose a lower maximum.',
    summary: `Part 4.30.4 addresses high-magnitude fault protection, not normal motor overload. Section 4.30.4.1 states that the devices protect motor branch-circuit conductors, motor-control apparatus, and motors against overcurrent caused by short circuits or grounds and that these rules add to or amend Article 2.40. The same motor circuit therefore normally contains both overload protection under Part 4.30.3 and short-circuit/ground-fault protection under Part 4.30.4. Their purposes, current bases, and permitted ratings differ.

Section 4.30.4.2(a) routes an individual motor circuit through its general, rating/setting, and special provisions. Subsection (b) adds a practical performance requirement: the protective device must be capable of carrying motor starting current. Subsection (c)(1) then requires a device rating or setting not exceeding the value calculated from Table 4.30.4.2. The calculation begins with the motor full-load current basis assigned by §4.30.1.6(a)(1), not automatically the motor nameplate FLC used for overload protection.

Table 4.30.4.2 differentiates device types and motor types. It does not authorize one universal “motor breaker percentage.” For example, the verified row for an ac polyphase squirrel-cage motor other than Design B energy-efficient shows different multipliers for a nontime-delay fuse, a dual-element time-delay fuse, an instantaneous-trip breaker, and an inverse-time breaker. The engineer must identify both axes correctly. An instantaneous-trip circuit breaker also has application restrictions: §4.30.4.2(c)(3) requires it to be adjustable and part of a listed combination motor controller with coordinated overload and fault protection in each conductor.

The section contains controlled flexibility for starting. If the table result does not correspond to a standard device size or setting, Exception No. 1 permits the next higher standard ampere rating within its conditions. If the resulting device is still insufficient for starting current, Exception No. 2 permits increases subject to device-specific ceilings. Those ceilings are not default selections. The design record should document the original table calculation, the starting problem, the selected increase, and confirmation that the applicable maximum was not exceeded.

Equipment-specific limits remain decisive. Section 4.30.4.2(c)(2) says a maximum shown in a manufacturer overload-relay table or otherwise marked on the equipment must not be exceeded even if the Code table would allow more. The remaining paragraphs address instantaneous-trip breakers, multispeed motors, power-electronic devices, listed self-protected combination controllers, motor short-circuit protectors, and torque motors. Available fault current, interrupting rating, controller combination rating, conductor protection, and grounding/bonding effectiveness are additional system checks; a percentage calculation alone never completes the design.`,
